mock-data-generator/node_modules/*
sample/*
.vscode/*
mock-data-generator/markers/*
batch-app/markers/*
/.idea/
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CardDemo test-suite generated outputs.
# WHY: these paths are PRODUCED by the runner scripts (scripts/build_test_*.sh,
# scripts/run_*.sh) and must never be committed. Assumptions: the policy is
# intentionally NARROW -- it names only generated artifacts, so it cannot mask a
# real source file. The mutable per-run test WORKSPACE and its loaded VSAM INDEX
# files are already created OUTSIDE the repo, under $TMPDIR by
# scripts/test_env.sh, so no workspace or index rule is needed here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiled COBOL programs / shared modules (CARDDEMO_BUILD_DIR default).
/build/
# JUnit XML + coverage reports (CARDDEMO_REPORTS_DIR default).
/reports/
# gcov line-coverage artifacts from the GnuCOBOL-transpiled C (--coverage build).
# Kept global (not just under /build/) so a manual gcov run in a source tree is
# also covered; the GnuCOBOL --save-temps intermediates themselves are written
# into CARDDEMO_BUILD_DIR (/build/, already ignored above), so no separate .c
# rule is needed here.
*.gcno
*.gcda
*.gcov
# Python-harness coverage data (coverage.py; parallel mode writes .coverage.*).
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
# pytest / Python bytecode caches.
.pytest_cache/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CardDemo AWS migration -- generated build output, IaC state, local env.
# WHY: the migration adds four build trees (services/, ui/, data-migration/,
# infra/) whose toolchains -- Maven, npm/Vite, Terraform and CPython -- all
# write generated bytes back INTO the working tree, and none of it is source.
# Assumptions: this set is intentionally NARROW for the same reason as the
# test-suite block above, naming only generated artifacts and secret-bearing
# local files, so it cannot mask a real source file. The state and env rules
# carry the extra weight. Database and Cognito seed-user passwords are generated
# at `terraform apply` time by the hashicorp/random provider and are recorded in
# Terraform state as PLAINTEXT, so one committed state file would disclose the
# entire credential set at once. No such file is committed today.
#
# Assumption about how much these rules can actually carry, stated so that no
# reader mistakes them for a control they are not. An ignore rule keeps a
# matching path out of `git status` and out of an unqualified `git add`, which
# removes the most likely way a state file or a local .env reaches a commit:
# somebody staging a directory without reading what was in it. It does NOT make
# committing one impossible, and three routes past it are ordinary rather than
# exotic. `git add -f` overrides any rule outright. A rule edited, narrowed or
# reordered later stops matching what it matches now, silently. And a path that
# is ALREADY TRACKED is unaffected by a rule added afterwards, because ignore
# rules govern untracked paths only. What these rules therefore provide is
# accidental-staging protection, and that is the whole of it.
#
# Trade-offs accepted, and where the hard enforcement belongs instead. The
# project's "no secrets committed to the repository" constraint is met primarily
# by generating every credential at `terraform apply` time straight into Secrets
# Manager, by keeping the per-environment tfvars limited to sizing and retention
# values, and by having deployment authenticate through short-lived federated
# role assumption rather than a stored key -- so there is no long-lived secret in
# the repository for a rule to have to catch. The remaining hard gate is a
# pre-merge one, and it does not live in this file and cannot:
# `.github/workflows/infra-ci.yml` stages every migration-owned tracked file and
# fails on gitleaks findings before merge. These ignore rules remain useful as
# accidental-staging protection; the scanner is the independent enforcement
# layer that still sees a secret deliberately force-added to the index.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Maven build output; each of the nine Maven modules under services/ gets its
# own target/. Assumptions: nine is the count of MODULES in the reactor -- the
# eight bounded-context services plus common-lib, the shared kernel they all
# depend on -- and not a count of services, of which there are eight. The two
# numbers are one apart and are easy to conflate, so the distinction is stated
# here. A tenth target/ also appears, at services/ itself: the aggregator POM
# binds the Checkstyle documentation gate to `validate`, and that plugin writes
# its checker and result files there on every build. So the rule covers ten
# directories in practice, and counting modules is still the right way to
# describe it.
# Assumptions: deliberately NOT root-anchored, unlike /build/ and /reports/
# above, because every target/ appears nested under services/ -- at each module
# and, as just noted, at the aggregator itself -- and never at the repository
# root, where a leading slash would match nothing.
target/

# npm dependency tree installed for the React SPA under ui/.
# Assumptions: the legacy `mock-data-generator/node_modules/*` rule at the top
# of this file is PATH-SCOPED to a tree this repository no longer carries, so
# it does not cover ui/ -- this unanchored rule is required, not redundant.
node_modules/

# Vite production bundle for the SPA, written by `npm run build` to ui/dist/.
# Assumptions: left unanchored for the same reason as target/ above -- the
# bundle lands under ui/, never at a root-level dist/.
dist/

# setuptools build outputs from the data-migration/ ETL package.
# WHY : Refactoring Rationale: added after observing the gap rather than
# predicting it. Building the ETL wheel -- which the container build does, and
# which a contributor does when reproducing that build locally -- leaves
# data-migration/build/ and data-migration/src/carddemo_migration.egg-info/
# behind in the working tree, and NEITHER was covered. The /build/ rule in the
# section above is root-anchored on purpose, because it names the COBOL test
# harness's own output directory, so it deliberately does not reach a nested
# build/; and no egg-info rule existed at all. The consequence was concrete: a
# `git add -A` after a local wheel build would have committed generated bytes,
# including a copy of the package metadata that then goes stale.
# Assumptions: both are unanchored, for the same reason target/ and dist/ are --
# they appear nested under data-migration/ and never at the repository root.
# The egg-info rule is a glob because setuptools derives the directory name
# from the DISTRIBUTION name, so it is carddemo_migration.egg-info today and
# would silently stop matching a literal rule if the distribution were renamed.
# Trade-offs: `build/` unanchored is broader than the one directory observed,
# and that breadth is accepted for consistency with target/ and dist/ above --
# a directory literally named build/ anywhere in this tree is generated output
# in every case, and the narrower alternative would need a new rule for each
# future package rather than covering them by construction.
build/
*.egg-info/

# Provider and module cache created by `terraform init` (infra/bootstrap/,
# infra/envs/dev/, infra/envs/prod/, plus any infra/modules/* inited locally).
# Alternatives Considered: ignoring all of infra/ was evaluated and rejected
# outright -- the .tf source under infra/ IS the deliverable, so a blanket rule
# would mask the very files the migration exists to ship. Naming only the
# generated cache directory keeps every rule in this file narrow enough that no
# source file can be hidden by it.
.terraform/

# Assumptions: .terraform.lock.hcl is deliberately NOT ignored. The rule above
# ends in a slash, so it matches the cache DIRECTORY only; the lock file is a
# sibling regular file and stays tracked on purpose, because it pins provider
# checksums and is what makes `terraform init` reproducible in CI and for
# every other operator.

# Terraform state, plus every backup and workspace-suffixed variant of it.
# Trade-offs: the second pattern is deliberately wider than a bare
# `terraform.tfstate` so that terraform.tfstate.backup and
# terraform.tfstate.<serial>.backup are caught as well. That extra breadth is
# worth accepting because the failure it prevents is disclosure of every
# credential the random provider generated, in plaintext, in a single file --
# while its cost is nil, since no tracked file here ends in .tfstate or
# .tfstate.<anything>.
# Alternatives Considered: writing this as the one glob `*.tfstate*` covers the
# same files in a single line, but its trailing `*` spans the dot too, so it
# also swallows unrelated names such as `x.tfstateignore`. The explicit pair
# matches the state file and its dot-suffixed variants and nothing else.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*

# Assumptions: *.tfvars is deliberately NOT ignored, even though ignoring tfvars
# is the usual Terraform convention. Here infra/envs/dev/terraform.tfvars and
# infra/envs/prod/terraform.tfvars are required deliverables carrying only
# sizing, capacity and retention parameters and never a secret value, so a
# *.tfvars rule would silently drop two files this migration has to ship.
#
# Assumptions: automatically loaded override files are a different contract.
# They are operator-local material generated by deploy.yml or by a workstation
# and may contain account-specific identifiers or resolved values; the tracked
# deliverables are named exactly `terraform.tfvars`, so these narrow suffixes do
# not hide them.
*.auto.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars.json

# Local environment overrides for the SPA and the ETL. Real values live only in
# AWS Secrets Manager and SSM Parameter Store and are read at startup, so an
# .env on a developer's disk is untracked scratch by definition.
#
# Refactoring Rationale: this block previously named only `.env`, `.env.local`
# and `.env.*.local`, and that enumeration left a real hole. Vite resolves
# `.env.<mode>` and `.env.<mode>.local` for every build mode, so
# `ui/.env.production`, `ui/.env.development` and `ui/.env.staging` are files the
# toolchain actively looks for -- and none of the three old patterns matched any
# of them, because `.env.*.local` requires the `.local` suffix. A developer who
# populated `ui/.env.production` with a real API endpoint, pool identifier or
# credential would therefore have had it staged by `git add .` with nothing to
# stop it. The pair below closes that by inverting the rule: EVERYTHING with an
# `.env` stem is ignored, and the one file that has to stay tracked is named
# back in explicitly.
# Trade-offs: `.env.*` is deliberately broader than the names Vite itself
# resolves, so it also swallows `.env.bak`, `.env.prod` and any other suffix
# someone invents. That breadth is the point -- an ignore rule that has to be
# widened every time a new suffix appears is a rule that is already out of date
# -- and its only cost is that a NEW tracked deliverable with an `.env` stem
# would need a negation of its own, which the two below already demonstrate how
# to write.
.env
.env.*

# Assumptions: ui/.env.example is a required deliverable -- it documents variable
# NAMES with no values -- and has to stay tracked. Unlike the previous revision
# of this file, where these negations were a forward-looking guard, they are now
# LOAD-BEARING: `.env.*` above matches `.env.example` directly, so removing
# either line would untrack a file the migration is required to ship. The first
# covers a template at the repository root; the second covers one at any depth,
# which is where the only current instance lives (ui/.env.example).
# Assumptions: a negation cannot resurrect a file inside an ignored DIRECTORY,
# but that does not apply here -- every pattern in this block matches files, so
# both negations take effect.
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Refactoring Rationale: `__pycache__/` and `*.py[cod]` are deliberately NOT
# repeated in this block. Both already exist in the MI-06 block above and both
# are unanchored, so they already cover the new data-migration/ package; a
# second copy would duplicate a rule without changing any behaviour.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CardDemo AWS migration -- local interpreters and saved Terraform plans.
# WHY: the two blocks above cover generated build output, provider caches,
# state and local env files, but they leave two further families of generated
# bytes trackable, and each one is named as already-ignored by a file that
# ships in this repository -- so the omission made those two statements false,
# not merely incomplete. data-migration/requirements.txt tells an operator to
# create the interpreter at the repository root and asserts that .venv, venv
# and env are ignored at any depth; infra/modules/ecs-service/versions.tf
# asserts that this file excludes .terraform/, *.tfstate* and *.tfplan. Both
# assertions are now true. The narrowness promised by the two blocks above is
# preserved: every rule below names a generated artifact and none of them
# matches a tracked path (verified against `git ls-files`), so the tracked
# tfvars, the .terraform.lock.hcl files, ui/.env.example and every .tf, .py,
# .java and .yml source file stay exactly as tracked as they are today.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python virtual environments. The COBOL parity harness and the data-migration
# ETL share one interpreter created at the repository root, so a venv is always
# generated bytes: thousands of installed files, compiled extension modules and
# absolute interpreter paths that are valid on exactly one machine.
# Assumptions: no tracked path in this repository has a directory component
# named .venv, venv or env, so none of the three rules can mask a source file.
# The infra/envs/ tree is spelled with a trailing s and is therefore untouched
# by the `env/` rule.
# Trade-offs: `env/` is the broadest name of the three and would be a poor rule
# in a repository that used env/ for configuration. Accepting it here is what
# makes the requirements.txt contract true rather than aspirational, and the
# trailing slash confines each rule to DIRECTORIES, so a regular file named
# `env` -- a shell fragment, say -- would still be tracked.
# Alternatives Considered: root-anchoring these as /.venv/ was evaluated and
# rejected, because requirements.txt documents any-depth coverage precisely so
# that an operator who creates an interpreter under data-migration/ instead of
# at the root cannot commit it by accident.
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Saved Terraform plan files, written by `terraform plan -out=<name>.tfplan`.
# A saved plan is not a diff summary: it embeds the resolved values of every
# attribute the apply will set, which for this stack includes the Aurora master
# password and the Cognito seed-user passwords the hashicorp/random provider
# generates. One committed plan therefore discloses the same credential set as
# one committed state file, which is why the pattern belongs to the same
# no-secrets-in-the-repository constraint the *.tfstate rules serve above.
# Refactoring Rationale: written as the explicit pair rather than the single
# glob `*.tfplan*`, exactly as the state rules above are, and for the same
# stated reason -- a trailing `*` spans the dot as well, so it would also
# swallow unrelated names such as `x.tfplanignore`.
# Trade-offs: the second pattern is deliberately wider than the saved binary
# plan alone so that the human-readable rendering produced by
# `terraform show -json <name>.tfplan > <name>.tfplan.json` is caught too. That
# rendering is strictly easier to read than the binary plan, so leaving it
# trackable while ignoring its source would protect the harder target and miss
# the easier one.
*.tfplan
*.tfplan.*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CardDemo AWS migration -- local credentials and private key material.
# Assumptions: public trust anchors remain source artifacts: no `*.pem`, `*.crt`
# or `*.cer` blanket rule exists, so the checked-in CA bundle needed for
# verify-full database TLS remains trackable. The patterns below name only
# private-key containers, conventional private-key filenames, local cloud
# credential stores and service-account credential exports.
# Trade-offs: ignore rules reduce accidental staging but cannot defeat
# `git add -f`; the gitleaks gate in infra-ci remains authoritative for content.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
id_rsa
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519

.aws/credentials
.aws/sso/cache/
.azure/
.config/gcloud/application_default_credentials.json
.config/gcloud/credentials.db
.docker/config.json
.config/containers/auth.json
*.kubeconfig

service-account*.json
service_account*.json
*-service-account.json
*_service_account.json
